==English==
{{wikipedia|Jumping the shark}}

===Etymology===
From a moment on the TV series ''[[w:Happy Days|Happy Days]]'' in which the character Fonzie jumped over a shark on waterskis, and from then on (supposedly) the show wasn't as good.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|inf=[[jump]] [[the]] [[shark]]|jumps the shark|jumping the shark|jumped the shark}}

# {{idiom|of a [[television program]] or other [[narrative]]}} To undergo a [[storyline]] development which is so [[exceptional]] that all content following is [[disappointing]].
#: ''Happy Days '''jumped the shark''' when Fonzie jumped over a shark on waterskis.''
#* '''2003''', Jon Hein, ''Jump the Shark'', <span class="plainlinks">[http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=TPyzvSlPEwsC&q=%22jump+the+shark%22&dq=%22jump+the+shark%22&lr=&num=100&as_brr=0&ei=aFVXS9O7H4T-ywTP-fiQCw&cd=1 book overview]</span> (revised edition; Plume; ISBN 0452284104, 9780452284104)
#*: ''Happy Days'' infamously {{q|jumped the shark}} when Fonzie literally jumped a shark on water skis. ''I Love Lucy'' {{q|jumped the shark}} when Lucy and Ricky moved to the suburbs. ''The Brady Bunch'' {{q|jumped the shark}} when Cousin Oliver moved in.
#* '''2004''', Ronn Owens, ''Voice of Reason: Why the Left and Right are Wrong'',
#*: From that point on, ''Dallas'' was never the same. It hit its peak. It lost credibility. It {{q|jumped the shark}}.
#* '''2007''', [[w:Jeph Jacques|Jeph Jacques]], ''[http://questionablecontent.net/view.php?comic=951 Time To Add A Cute Kid To The Cast]'' Questionable Content Number 951
#*: Pintsize: Wait, don’t you want to know why I’m tied up and hanging from the ceiling?
#*: Faye: Not really.  Nighty night!
#*: Pintsize: Shit! My wacky antics have {{q|jumped the shark}}!
#* '''2008''', Mark Fogarty, ''Went to see the Gypsy'',
#*: The Bangles were a fine all-woman band, starting with a lovely cover of Prince's "Manic Monday." "Walk Like an Egyptian" was cute, and the ladies rocked out an unlikely cover of Simon and Garfunkel's "Hazy Shade of Winter." But they {{q|jumped the shark}} with the treacly "Eternal Flame," and the end of the road was in sight.
#* '''2008''' October 11, ''Billboard'' magazine,
#*: And I knew something was up when I started getting press releases about "tape labels." Maybe vinyl had finally {{q|jumped the shark}}, and magnetic tape was due for a return.

